\section{Validation Methodology}
\label{sec:direction-validation}

The estimator is evaluated on the same directional records used by the marine
motion study: four JONSWAP seas and four Pierson--Moskowitz seas, each passed through the v1.2.1
28 ft vessel RAO.  Each record is \SI{1200}{s} long at \SI{200}{Hz}; scoring
uses the final \SI{900}{s} so that startup and magnetic-reference acquisition
do not dominate the direction metric.  Ten paired seed triplets vary the wave
realization, sensor noise and drift, and residual-error initialization.  The
direction evidence is simulation based; it is not yet a comparison against a
physical directional wave buoy.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Stationary direction-validation scenarios.}
\label{tab:direction-scenarios}
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrr@{}}
\toprule
Scenario & $H_s$ [m] & $T_p$ [s] & propagation-to azimuth \\
\midrule
W1 & 0.27 & 3.0  & $+30^\circ$ \\
W2 & 1.50 & 5.7  & $-30^\circ$ \\
W3 & 4.00 & 8.5  & $+30^\circ$ \\
W4 & 8.50 & 11.4 & $-30^\circ$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The propagation-plane estimate is axial, so its error is evaluated modulo
$180^\circ$.  For truth axis $\theta$ and estimate $\hat\theta$, define
\begin{equation}
e_{\rm axis}=\frac12\left|\wrap_{(-\pi,\pi]}
\left(2(\hat\theta-\theta)\right)\right|.
\label{eq:axis-error}
\end{equation}
Once travel sense is resolved, the directed propagation-to vector is scored
modulo $360^\circ$.  Travel sense is counted as correct, wrong, or unresolved
against the generator's physical propagation direction.  The implementation's
FORWARD/BACKWARD labels themselves are not treated as physical truth: they are
labels relative to whichever axial representative the estimator returns and
can exchange names when that representative flips by $180^\circ$.
All ten replays are retained. Angular scores are conditional on a finite
estimate; a replay with no resolved travel direction contributes to the
unresolved fraction but has no directed angular score. Summary counts and
paired counts use finite observations and their matched seed intersection.

\subsection{Known-vessel response matching}
The direction branch receives the engine-guarded accelerometer. After levelling,
a direction-only equalizer matches surge, sway and heave amplitude and phase.
In the causal Laplace convention, let
\begin{equation}
Q_j(s)=(1+\tau_j s)^{-2},\qquad
G_h(s)=\left(1+2\zeta_hs/\omega_h+s^2/\omega_h^2\right)^{-1}.
\end{equation}
For the pinned hull, $\tau_{\rm surge}=0.7$ s, $\tau_{\rm sway}=1.0$ s,
$\omega_h=2\pi/2.4$ rad/s and $\zeta_h=0.45$. With
$Q_t=(1+s)^{-2}$, the compensators are $E_j=Q_t/Q_j$ and $E_z=Q_t/G_h$.
They are stable and proper, and their magnitudes do not exceed one.
The common footprint/depth attenuation remains; this is relative response
matching, not incident-wave-height reconstruction. Matching heave as well as
both horizontal channels preserves the orbital quadrature needed for travel
sense. The existing simulation basis conversion exchanges surge and sway at
the input of this stage, so the channel time constants are assigned accordingly.

The wave-period tuner reads the pre-equalized signal. Neither attitude nor
translation receives equalizer output. The profile is explicitly enabled in
the vessel simulators; generic deployment requires a known response in the
correct coordinate frame. Weak response, response nulls and directional spread
can still leave direction unresolved. Confidence and simulation acceptance
thresholds are unchanged. The v1.2.1 phase kernel
$\cos(\mathbf{k}\cdot\mathbf{x}-\omega t+\phi)$ defines the reported azimuth as
propagation-to, with no additional $180^\circ$ shift.

