\subsection{Frequency and confidence interpretation}

KalmANF supplies the carrier frequency used to advance the quadrature phase.
The direction estimator does not require its carrier phase to be correct,
because both sine and cosine quadratures are fitted and combined through the
phase-independent moment matrix in Eq.~\eqref{eq:dir-moment}.  Frequency error
and sea nonstationarity are nevertheless omitted from the first-order angular
uncertainty in Eq.~\eqref{eq:dir-axis-uncertainty}; the reported confidence is
therefore a local estimator diagnostic rather than a calibrated frequentist
coverage probability.

\section{Discussion and Limitations}
\label{sec:direction-discussion}

The method estimates a dominant propagation plane and its apparent travel sense
from a single IMU plus an attitude/heading frame.  It does not reconstruct a
full directional spectrum $S(f,\theta)$, and crossing seas with comparable
energy in multiple directions can reduce the linearity statistic or cause the
principal axis to represent only the dominant component.  Low-energy motion is
handled by amplitude, covariance, linearity, and phase-coherence gates rather
than by forcing a direction output.

The direct result is an encounter, boat-relative direction.  Converting it to
an intrinsic water-relative direction requires vessel velocity through water
and a dispersion model; converting further to a ground-referenced phase
velocity requires current and heading in a common earth-fixed frame, as shown
in Sec.~\ref{sec:direction}.  Following-sea overtaking can reverse the apparent
phase progression, so encounter and intrinsic travel sense are not assumed to
be identical in that regime.

The present validation uses synthetic directional seas and the committed
sensor-error model.  Dedicated experiments should next sweep vessel heading,
propagation azimuth, crossing-sea mixtures, current, vessel speed, and tracker
policy, followed by comparison against a directional wave buoy or wave-tank
reference.  The alternative Aranovskiy and PLL trackers in
Appendix~\ref{app:tracker-alternatives} are implementation options only; the
current evidence does not rank them against KalmANF.

Without response matching, unequal surge/sway gains and heave phase distort
the incident direction signature. The known-preset equalizer addresses this
relative distortion, while retained footprint attenuation, directional spread,
attitude error and sensor noise still limit accuracy and availability. It does
not establish validity for an unknown hull or recover information at response
nulls.
